{
  "id": "cb2",
  "title": "Working-Storage",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "cb2-l1",
      "title": "Fields and PIC",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Data lives in the DATA DIVISION, in its WORKING-STORAGE SECTION. Every field is declared with a level number (01 for now), a name and a PIC clause, its picture.",
        "PIC X(10) is a text field of exactly 10 characters. PIC 9(5) holds 5 digits. VALUE gives the field its start value.",
        "A field always has its full size: a 10-character name that holds ADA is ADA and 7 spaces. That's why the example shows the brackets."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
          "       PROGRAM-ID. FIELDS.",
          "       DATA DIVISION.",
          "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
          "       01 CUSTOMER    PIC X(10) VALUE \"ADA\".",
          "       01 CITY        PIC X(6)  VALUE \"LONDON\".",
          "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
          "           DISPLAY \"[\" CUSTOMER \"]\".",
          "           DISPLAY \"CITY: \" CITY.",
          "           STOP RUN."
        ],
        "output": [
          "[ADA       ]",
          "CITY: LONDON"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "cb2-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does PIC X(8) describe?",
          "options": [
            "A text field of 8 characters",
            "A number with 8 digits",
            "8 text fields",
            "A field that can hold at most 8"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "X means any character, (8) means 8 of them. It always has 8."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb2-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this job show?",
          "code": [
            "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
            "       PROGRAM-ID. PAD.",
            "       DATA DIVISION.",
            "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
            "       01 W PIC X(5) VALUE \"HI\".",
            "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
            "           DISPLAY \"[\" W \"]\".",
            "           STOP RUN."
          ],
          "options": [
            "[HI   ]",
            "[HI]",
            "[   HI]",
            "HI"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The field has 5 characters: HI and 3 spaces."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb2-l1-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Declare a field for a 20-character name.",
          "code": [
            "       01 NAME  PIC ___ ."
          ],
          "options": [
            "X(20)",
            "9(20)",
            "20X",
            "TEXT(20)"
          ],
          "answer": "X(20)",
          "explanation": "X for text, the size in brackets."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb2-l1-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Where are fields declared?",
          "options": [
            "In the WORKING-STORAGE SECTION of the DATA DIVISION",
            "In the PROCEDURE DIVISION",
            "In the IDENTIFICATION DIVISION",
            "Anywhere"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Data and statements are kept apart in COBOL."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb2-l1-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This job doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
            "       PROGRAM-ID. LONG.",
            "       DATA DIVISION.",
            "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
            "       01 CODE-NAME PIC X(3) VALUE \"MAINFRAME\".",
            "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
            "           DISPLAY CODE-NAME.",
            "           STOP RUN."
          ],
          "answer": 4,
          "explanation": "MAINFRAME has 9 characters, but the field only has room for 3."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb2-l1-e6",
          "type": "order_lines",
          "prompt": "Put the lines in order.",
          "lines": [
            "       DATA DIVISION.",
            "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
            "       01 GREETING PIC X(5) VALUE \"HELLO\".",
            "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
            "           DISPLAY GREETING."
          ],
          "answer": [
            "       DATA DIVISION.",
            "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
            "       01 GREETING PIC X(5) VALUE \"HELLO\".",
            "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
            "           DISPLAY GREETING."
          ],
          "explanation": "The division, its section, the field, then the procedure that uses it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cb2-l2",
      "title": "Numbers in PIC 9",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "PIC 9(4) holds a number with exactly 4 digits. The value 42 is stored as 0042, and that's what DISPLAY shows: COBOL never hides the leading zeros of a PIC 9 field.",
        "That fits business forms, where an account number is always 6 digits: 004711.",
        "A value that has more digits than the picture won't even compile. Text in quotes can't go into a PIC 9 field either."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
          "       PROGRAM-ID. ACCOUNT.",
          "       DATA DIVISION.",
          "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
          "       01 ACCOUNT-NO  PIC 9(6) VALUE 4711.",
          "       01 BRANCH      PIC 9(3) VALUE 7.",
          "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
          "           DISPLAY \"ACCOUNT \" ACCOUNT-NO.",
          "           DISPLAY \"BRANCH  \" BRANCH.",
          "           STOP RUN."
        ],
        "output": [
          "ACCOUNT 004711",
          "BRANCH  007"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "cb2-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this job show?",
          "code": [
            "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
            "       PROGRAM-ID. ZEROS.",
            "       DATA DIVISION.",
            "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
            "       01 N PIC 9(4) VALUE 42.",
            "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
            "           DISPLAY N.",
            "           STOP RUN."
          ],
          "options": [
            "0042",
            "42",
            "  42",
            "4200"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "PIC 9(4) always has 4 digits, with zeros in front."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb2-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How many digits does PIC 999 hold?",
          "options": [
            "3",
            "999",
            "9",
            "1"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "999 is the same as 9(3)."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb2-l2-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Declare a 5-digit customer number.",
          "code": [
            "       01 CUST-NO  PIC ___ VALUE 123."
          ],
          "options": [
            "9(5)",
            "X(5)",
            "5(9)",
            "N(5)"
          ],
          "answer": "9(5)",
          "explanation": "9 for digits."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb2-l2-e4",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This job doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
            "       PROGRAM-ID. BIG.",
            "       DATA DIVISION.",
            "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
            "       01 YEAR-NO PIC 99 VALUE 1959.",
            "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
            "           DISPLAY YEAR-NO.",
            "           STOP RUN."
          ],
          "answer": 4,
          "explanation": "1959 has 4 digits, but PIC 99 holds only 2. Use PIC 9(4)."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb2-l2-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why does COBOL show 004711 and not 4711?",
          "options": [
            "A PIC 9(6) field always has 6 digits",
            "It's an error",
            "It's in octal",
            "COBOL can't remove zeros"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Later you'll learn edited pictures that do hide them."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb2-l2-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Which value can go into PIC 9(3)?",
          "options": [
            "725",
            "\"725\"",
            "7250",
            "-7"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Three digits, no quotes, no sign."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cb2-l3",
      "title": "MOVE",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "MOVE copies a value into a field: MOVE \"KEN\" TO CUSTOMER. The old value is gone. MOVE … TO can even fill several fields at once.",
        "Text is moved left to right: it's filled up with spaces, and if it's too long, the end is cut off.",
        "Numbers are moved right to left, by their digits: if a number is too big, its front digits are cut off, without any warning. MOVE 1234 TO a PIC 99 field leaves 34. Banks have lost money this way."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
          "       PROGRAM-ID. MOVES.",
          "       DATA DIVISION.",
          "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
          "       01 CUSTOMER   PIC X(8).",
          "       01 SMALL      PIC 99.",
          "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
          "           MOVE \"KEN\" TO CUSTOMER.",
          "           DISPLAY \"[\" CUSTOMER \"]\".",
          "           MOVE \"KERNIGHAN AND RITCHIE\" TO CUSTOMER.",
          "           DISPLAY \"[\" CUSTOMER \"]\".",
          "           MOVE 1234 TO SMALL.",
          "           DISPLAY \"SMALL: \" SMALL.",
          "           MOVE 7 TO SMALL.",
          "           DISPLAY \"SMALL: \" SMALL.",
          "           STOP RUN."
        ],
        "output": [
          "[KEN     ]",
          "[KERNIGHA]",
          "SMALL: 34",
          "SMALL: 07"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "cb2-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this job show?",
          "code": [
            "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
